From: Kyvenza <support@kyvenza.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 15:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] ui/vnc: enlarge the client socket send buffer

A FramebufferUpdate for a 2560x1600 desktop is 0.5-1 MiB even with Tight,
and 3-8 MiB with Raw.  The VNC server writes it from the main loop with the
platform default send buffer, which is 8 KiB for AF_UNIX sockets on macOS:
hundreds of writes per update, each waiting for the socket to become
writable again.  Tracing the guest shows it flushing ~55 times/s while the
client only sees ~16 updates/s, because refresh ticks that fire while the
previous update is still draining find the client busy and are skipped.

Ask for the largest send buffer the kernel will grant when a client
connects, trying 32 MiB and halving down to 1 MiB.  A Raw update for a
2560x1600 desktop is 16 MiB; the fewer writes it takes, the fewer main loop
iterations it waits for.  On macOS kern.ipc.maxsockbuf is 8 MiB by default
and setsockopt() fails outright above it rather than clamping, so a single
fixed request either wastes headroom or silently leaves the 8 KiB default
in place.  A failure on the last rung is harmless.

Signed-off-by: Kyvenza <support@kyvenza.com>
---
--- a/ui/vnc.c
+++ b/ui/vnc.c
@@ -3306,6 +3306,21 @@
                         bool skipauth, bool websocket)
 {
     VncConnection *vc = g_new0(VncConnection, 1);
+    /*
+     * A framebuffer update for a large desktop is several MiB.  With the
+     * platform default send buffer (8 KiB for AF_UNIX on macOS) that is
+     * hundreds of writes, each needing another main loop iteration, and
+     * every refresh tick that lands in between finds the client still busy.
+     * Ask for as much as the kernel will grant, from 32 MiB down.  macOS
+     * rejects (rather than clamps) anything above kern.ipc.maxsockbuf, 8 MiB
+     * by default, so a single fixed request would either waste the headroom
+     * or silently keep the 8 KiB default.  Failing every rung is harmless.
+     */
+    for (size_t sndbuf = 32 * 1024 * 1024; sndbuf >= 1024 * 1024; sndbuf /= 2) {
+        if (qio_channel_socket_set_send_buffer(sioc, sndbuf, NULL) == 0) {
+            break;
+        }
+    }
     VncState *vs = &vc->vs;
     bool first_client = QTAILQ_EMPTY(&vd->clients);
 
